# Coding standards

## General

- Prefer clarity over cleverness; name things for their role
- Keep functions small and single-purpose
- Handle errors explicitly; never swallow exceptions silently
- Validate all external input (API, forms, query params) before use
- No secrets in source; use environment variables

## Style

- Consistent formatting via project tooling (formatter/linter when present)
- Prefer early returns over deep nesting
- Avoid unnecessary abstraction until a second real use appears
- Comments explain *why*, not what the code already says

## UI / web

- Semantic HTML; meaningful labels and alt text
- Keyboard-accessible interactive elements
- Mobile-responsive by default
- Prefer CSS variables for brand color/type tokens
- Follow `design-system.mdc`: two tones plus one accent, no gradients, no
  borders, no shadows. Separate with spacing and type, not lines

## APIs & data

- Schema-validate request/response bodies
- Use structured logging with context (no PII in logs)
- Paginate large collections; index hot query paths
- Fail gracefully for non-critical dependencies

## Tests

- Cover core business logic and failure paths
- Mock external services
- Run relevant tests before shipping risky changes
